             <span class="problem-title">R3 · Automatic differentiation without a step</span>
             <p>Explain why the step-balancing argument of Proposition 4.2 does not apply to a method that computes the derivative without a difference quotient, and name the lesson of this unit that treats such a method.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>The argument needs a step \(h\) and a difference quotient, and it trades truncation against round-off through \(h\). A method that computes the derivative without a quotient has no step to choose, so the trade-off in Proposition 4.2 does not arise for it. The method is treated in Lesson L14, where forward-mode automatic differentiation is defined. This page makes no claim about its accuracy.</p>
